 "news": {
  "date": "2026-10-16",
  "asof": "2026-10-17 18:28 UTC",
  "headline": "The Duo sold out in minutes \u2014 and everything else in the book was sold with the AI trade",
  "standfirst": "Apple opened iPhone Duo pre-orders at 8am Eastern and sold out within moments, calling demand \"nothing short of extraordinary\"; shipping estimates slipped from late October to November to December and, on some configurations, into late January 2027, with units reselling at up to $5,000 against a $1,999 list. AAPL rose 0.43% to 343.46 and was the only holding to gain. Everything else went with the AI complex, and this time not because of rates: the Nasdaq fell 2.15% and the S&P 1.09% while the Dow fell 0.34% and the ten-year rose two basis points, with Micron \u22125.6% and Intel \u22125.8% on doubts about how long AI infrastructure spending lasts. Both the S&P and the Nasdaq had their worst week since June \u2014 and the Dow was the only index up on the week, at +0.7%. In the book OKTA fell 10.20%, its largest ever single session, taking it to +0.23R from +2.46R a week ago, and GOOG fell 2.32% to its lowest since 2 October. A net of -27.80, and -83.62 across the week. Crude broke $100 \u2014 WTI $101.41, Brent $109.86 after touching $110 \u2014 through an intercepted missile aimed at Yanbu and roughly 37m barrels of announced emergency release. Heat 3.05R against 3.0R, unchanged for a twenty-seventh session.",
  "themes": [
   {
    "kicker": "What's next",
    "right": "the Duo ships the 23rd \u00b7 then the Fed and three prints",
    "title": "Ten days of nothing, then everything at once",
    "items": [
     {
      "tone": "c-er",
      "tag": "AAPL",
      "html": "<b>iPhone Duo first shipments</b> and retail availability in the US and 70-plus countries, with 28 more on the 30th \u2014 the test is whether store stock lasts the morning",
      "when": "Fri \u00b7 2026-10-23",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-fomc",
      "tag": "FOMC",
      "html": "<b>Fed decision</b> \u2014 roughly an 85% hold now priced after Warsh's IMF remarks, with December the expected hike",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "GOOG",
      "html": "<b>Alphabet Q3 earnings</b>, the same day as the Fed \u2014 $195-205bn of capex into a market that spent Friday questioning AI spending",
      "when": "Wed \u00b7 2026-10-28",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "AAPL \u00b7 AMZN",
      "html": "<b>Apple Q4 and Amazon Q3 earnings</b> \u2014 Apple's with the foldable already sold out, Amazon's the first read on holiday demand",
      "when": "Thu \u00b7 2026-10-29",
      "kind": "calendar"
     },
     {
      "tone": "c-cb",
      "tag": "CPI",
      "html": "<b>October CPI</b> \u2014 with crude having gone from $90.76 to $101.41 inside the month",
      "when": "Tue \u00b7 2026-11-10",
      "kind": "calendar"
     }
    ],
    "note": "<b>There is nothing dated between now and the 23rd, and that is worth saying plainly because it has been three weeks since that was true.</b> No print, no earnings, no scheduled event from any company held. The Duo's first shipments and retail availability land <b>Friday 23 October</b>, and the only checkable thing then is whether store stock survives the morning. Then the calendar compresses hard: the <b>Fed and Alphabet on the 28th</b>, <b>Apple and Amazon on the 29th</b>, Palantir on <b>2 November</b> and the October CPI on the <b>10th</b>."
   }
  ],
  "ondeck": "<b>There is nothing dated between now and the 23rd, and that is worth saying plainly because it has been three weeks since that was true.</b> No print, no earnings, no scheduled event from any company held. The Duo's first shipments and retail availability land <b>Friday 23 October</b>, and the only checkable thing then is whether store stock survives the morning. Then the calendar compresses hard: the <b>Fed and Alphabet on the 28th</b>, <b>Apple and Amazon on the 29th</b>, Palantir on <b>2 November</b> and the October CPI on the <b>10th</b>.",
